            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"UNPERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"NONE\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book's core identification that the 'new moon' is the full moon relies on collapsing 'new moon' (chodesh) and 'full moon' (keseh) in Psalm 81:3 into a single day, and associating the new moon with the east gate in Ezekiel 46:1 because the full moon rises in the east. However, Ezekiel 46:1 states the east gate is opened on both the new moon and the Sabbath. Since the weekly Sabbath occurs on all moon phases, the gate's opening accommodates the Prince's and people's worship, not the astronomical rising of the full moon. Furthermore, equating the start of the month (new moon) with the full moon severely disrupts the biblical calendar, which places the blowing of trumpets on the 1st of the month (new moon) and solemn feasts like Tabernacles on the 15th. The counter-interpretation recognizes Hebrew poetic parallelism in Psalm 81:3 referencing the 1st-and-15th festival cycle, explaining the lexical distinctions and maintaining calendrical coherence without contradiction.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The 'new moon' is the full moon phase, which marks the start of the biblical month and enters the east gate.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"The renewed moon\",\n    \"sign of the appointed times\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [\n    \"The renewed moon\",\n    \"the phase of the moon that marks the start of the month\",\n    \"the full moon in the east gate\"\n  ],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The typological association of the full moon with the 'pearl of great price' and the bride of Christ is not printed in the glossary entry.\",\n    \"The claim that the full moon is the 'throne' (keseh vs kisse) relies on unpointed Hebrew punning without sufficient contextual necessity.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"The 'new moon' (chodesh) refers to the start of the lunar month (traditionally the first visible crescent or conjunction), distinct from the full moon (keseh) which occurs mid-month.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"The new moon is the beginning of the lunar month (crescent or conjunction), distinguishing it from the full moon.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The new moon is not the full moon, and the full moon does not mark the start of the month.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"In the biblical calendar, the month begins with the new moon (Rosh Chodesh, e.g., Feast of Trumpets on the 1st day of the 7th month), while major solemn feasts (Tabernacles, Passover) occur on the 15th day, which naturally aligns with the full moon.\",\n    \"Psalm 81:3 uses standard Hebrew poetic parallelism to encompass this festival month: blowing the trumpet on the new moon (the 1st) and at the full moon for the solemn feast (the 15th). The absence of the conjunction 'and' is a routine feature of Hebrew poetic parallelism, not proof of identity.\",\n    \"Ezekiel 46:1 commands the east gate to be opened on both the Sabbath and the new moon. Since the Sabbath occurs every seven days regardless of the moon's phase, the gate's opening is tied to scheduled communal worship days, not the physical rising of a full moon in the east.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Leverages the absence of the 'waw' conjunction in Psalm 81:3 to read 'new moon' and 'full moon' in apposition as the same event.\",\n    \"Connects the east gate of Ezekiel 46 to the astronomical observation that the full moon is the phase that rises in the east as the sun sets.\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Maintains the established distinction between the Hebrew terms 'chodesh' (new moon/month) and 'keseh' (full moon).\",\n    \"Fits the biblical calendrical structure (Leviticus 23, Numbers 29) where the 1st day (new moon) and 15th day (major feast) are distinct appointed times.\",\n    \"Explains Ezekiel 46:1's inclusion of the Sabbath alongside the new moon without forcing an impossible astronomical correlation (since Sabbaths do not align exclusively with full moons rising in the east).\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [\n    \"Ezekiel 46:1 states the east gate is opened on the Sabbath as well as the new moon; the Sabbath does not correspond to an eastern-rising full moon.\",\n    \"If the start of the month (1st day) is the full moon, then the major biblical feasts commanded for the 15th day of the month would occur during the dark conjunction, contradicting the well-lit nature of the 'keseh' (full moon) feast day mentioned in Psalm 81:3.\"\n  ],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"COUNTER\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Psalm 81:3\",\n    \"Ezekiel 46:1\",\n    \"Implied calendrical texts referencing the 1st and 15th days (e.g., Leviticus 23)\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
